---
import Journey from '../../layouts/Journey.astro';
import Step from '../../components/journey/Step.astro';
import Next from '../../components/journey/Next.astro';
import More from '../../components/journey/More.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/site/CopyPrompt.astro';
import ForLevel from '../../components/level/ForLevel.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/nav';
---
<Journey
  chapter="first"
  first="setup"
  title="Your first real loop"
  description="A calm, practical bridge from idea to your first working app on a laptop, desktop PC or phone—no boilerplate overload."
>
  <Step id="setup" title="Pick your starting device">
    <p>You do not need a complicated developer setup to begin. Choose what is in front of you right now:</p>
    <div class="pick" role="group" aria-label="Starting device">
      <button type="button" data-do="setup" data-val="laptop" data-default>Laptop</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="setup" data-val="desktop">Desktop PC</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="setup" data-val="phone">Phone first</button>
    </div>
    <ForLevel is="plain">
      <p>On a laptop or desktop PC, create one empty folder, open it in a coding agent app (like T3 Code, Claude Code or Codex), and sign in with your normal AI account. On a phone, record a short voice note of what you want or spin up a quick prototype in a web builder.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="technical">
      <p>Run <code>git init</code> in a clean folder, open it in T3 Code / Claude Code / Codex, and make sure Node (or your chosen runtime) is installed so the agent can run typechecks and unit tests locally.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="engineer">
      <p>Bootstrap a minimal repo with strict TypeScript, Vitest, a single <code>verify</code> script (<code>tsc --noEmit &amp;&amp; vitest run</code>) and a 15-line <code>AGENTS.md</code>—this guarantees every agent loop has a fast, deterministic oracle from turn one.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <div id="builders">
      <More title="Side story: zero-setup web builders (v0, Lovable, Bolt, Replit)">
        <p><a href={url('wiki/web-builders/')} data-side>Web builders</a> like v0, Lovable, Bolt and Replit are the fastest possible first taste: open a browser tab, describe an app, and click a live page in two minutes with nothing installed. Treat them as an easy on-ramp—once a project matters or grows past a prototype, export it to GitHub and bring it into a real coding agent where you own the files, tests and architecture.</p>
      </More>
    </div>
  </Step>

  <Step id="project" title="Pick one small, real job">
    <p>Skip “build a social network.” Pick something useful that fits on one screen—tap an example to see the prompt at your reading level:</p>
    <div class="pick" role="group" aria-label="Starter project">
      <button type="button" data-do="idea" data-val="club" data-default>Club members list</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="idea" data-val="notes">Invoice maker</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="idea" data-val="tool">CSV expense grouper</button>
    </div>
    <ForLevel is="plain">
      <CopyPrompt title="Starter prompt (copy and adapt)" compact>Build a simple web page for our 40-person club:
- Show each member's name, whether they paid this season, and a search box.
- Include 20 realistic sample members so I can try it right away.
- Tell me the link to open it and confirm search and filtering both work.</CopyPrompt>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="technical">
      <CopyPrompt title="Starter prompt with tests & typecheck" compact>Scaffold a small Vite + TypeScript web app for a club members list (search + paid filter + 20 fixture records).
- Put filter and summary logic in src/members.ts with Vitest unit tests.
- Run tsc --noEmit and vitest run and fix any errors before reporting done.
- Commit the clean baseline to git.</CopyPrompt>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="engineer">
      <CopyPrompt title="Starter prompt with clean boundaries" compact>Bootstrap a minimal strict-TypeScript Vite app for a club members tracker:
- Pure domain module (src/members.ts) with strict types and unit tests covering empty/edge states.
- Thin UI layer with zero unnecessary dependencies.
- Add a concise AGENTS.md documenting stack, folder boundaries and the exact verify command (tsc --noEmit &amp;&amp; vitest run).
- Run verify, confirm green, and commit the baseline.</CopyPrompt>
    </ForLevel>
  </Step>

  <Step id="run" title="Watch the four-beat loop">
    <ForLevel is="plain">
      <p>Describe what you want → the agent creates the files → it checks that the page works → you open the live page and click through it. That simple loop is the heartbeat of every chapter ahead.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="technical">
      <p>Prompt → inspect &amp; edit → run <code>tsc --noEmit</code> and unit tests → verify the live UI. Never skip beat 3 or beat 4.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="engineer">
      <p>Every advanced pattern in Parts 2 and 3—memory files, subagents, git worktrees, cross-model review and remote machines—optimizes one stage of this four-beat loop.</p>
    </ForLevel>
  </Step>

  <Step id="tweak" title="Make one specific tweak—then go under the hood">
    <p>The first version is only a draft. Open it, pick one concrete improvement, and ask for just that:</p>
    <div class="row">
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-do="tweak">Ask for one tweak</button>
    </div>
    <ForLevel is="plain">
      <p>Once you have done two or three small tweaks, you already have the feel of agentic coding. Part 2 opens the hood and shows how to grow from this one chat to twenty agents working together.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="technical">
      <p>Small, single-purpose follow-up prompts keep diffs tight, tests green and context windows clean. Part 2 unpacks what happens inside the window, why long threads degrade, and how to scale safely.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="engineer">
      <p>With a reproducible single-agent baseline in place, Part 2 dives into context mechanics, attention rot, instruction architecture, verification gates and multi-agent orchestration.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <Next from="first" more={[{ href: 'library/', text: 'Browse all 21 lessons & concepts' }]} />
  </Step>
</Journey>
